# Vulnerability details

## Impact

A replayed call to the `updateBattleRecord` function by the game server will go through and will count as a new battle record.

## Description

Battle records have no uniqueness attributes (e.g. battle ID), neither an on-chain registry of battles (e.g. already/pending to be resolved, fighters involved, round), nor a deadline. Literally there is no data that can be used as a nonce by the `updateBattleRecord` function to reject already processed battle records. The consequences are that any mistake by the game server logic that ends up replaying a call to `updateBattleRecord` will count as a new battle record. This may involve the potential loss of data integrity concerning the token and its owner (e.g. points stats, stake at risk) across multiple contracts (e.g. `RankedBattle`, `StakeAtRisk`, `FighterFarm`), which also increases the risk of a DoS on future battle record updates. Another caveat is that the users have to trust the game server API/front-end data that maps battle record with transactions instead of verifying it on-chain.

